WHAT D7 WAS, ON THE CORE SIDE. The python slice's corpus run found that ak_rt's
unknown-field skip had no case for the deprecated GROUP form, so wire type 3 fell
through to AK_ERR_MALFORMED and the core rejected two corpus vectors that upb
accepts. R0 makes that the aggregating session's, and it is fixed in the shared
core. This slice loads libak_core.so as a file, so its obligation was: merge,
rebuild, confirm the load, re-gate. Done, and nothing moved.

WHAT IT WAS ON THIS SLICE'S SIDE, WHICH IS THE REASON THIS LOG EXISTS.
`Facade/Wire.cs`'s `Dec.Skip(int wire)` had the identical hole: cases for the
four wire types a proto3 schema produces and `ErrMalformed` for everything else.

**Byte identity against ffi/schema's manifest could not have found this and never
will.** Every payload there is emitted from the description the decoder is
emitted from, so no payload in it can carry a field the decoder does not know,
and proto3 cannot express a group at all. The slice's own hand-built
unknown-field vectors (`harness unknown`) cover wire types 0, 1, 2 and 5, because
those are the ones a proto3 writer can produce. The corpus is what found it.

THE FIX IS NOT "ADD CASE 3", and the reject vectors are why.

  A group carries no length. Its end is an END_GROUP tag whose FIELD NUMBER
  matches the one that opened it, so the skipper recurses rather than seeking.

  * A skipper that counts depth instead of matching the field number ACCEPTS
    X-group-mismatched-end and then mis-nests every group after it. That is a
    wrong parse, not a rejected one, and nothing downstream would notice.
  * A skipper that scans for an end tag without checking the buffer walks off
    the end of X-group-unterminated.
  * A recursive skipper with no depth bound answers a nest of start tags with a
    stack overflow, which is a crash and not an error. Bounded at 100, which is
    protobuf's own default recursion limit, reported as a new facade error code.

  Two error-code notes, both traps worth naming once. The facade's codes are its
  own and are NOT the ABI's: ak-rt numbers malformed -2 and depth -4, this
  numbers malformed -4, and nothing converts between them because the managed
  control does not go through the C ABI at all. And the new depth code is -8
  rather than -4, so it does not collide with the malformed code this slice has
  been printing in logs since stage 1.

THE DEFECT, SEEN FAILING. The fix was reverted once and the gate re-run, because
a guard with no failing test is a guard nobody has watched work.

