X-group-unterminated       10  reject  ok         ok       yes    a group that is never closed (corpus root WireZoo; read here under ListResultsResponse, where tag 120 is equally unknown)
X-group-mismatched-end     12  reject  ok         ok       yes    END_GROUP with a field number that does not match the group that opened (same substitution)

And the case no vector covers, because a corpus of malformed bytes has to
stop somewhere: 200 nested start tags, past the 100 the skipper allows. A
recursive skipper with no bound answers this with a stack overflow, which
is a crash and not a rejection.
  err = -4, EXPECTED ErrDepth (-8)
  Google.Protobuf: rejected, InvalidProtocolBufferException

7 failure(s)

  Note what the two X- rows say there: they PASSED without the fix, and they
  passed for the wrong reason. `ErrMalformed` on an unhandled wire type is not
  the same event as rejecting an unterminated group, and a reject vector that
  passes tells you nothing about why. Only the accept vectors showed the hole.

