READING THESE THREE, WHICH IS THE POINT OF RUNNING THEM.

**A -- counting depth instead of matching the END_GROUP's field number.** Caught
by `X-group-mismatched-end`, and by nothing else: `groups` reports 2 failures
while `conformance` reports 152 checks 0 failures and `unknown` reports 0. This
is the vector earning its place. A skipper that got here would accept the vector
and then attribute every subsequent group to the wrong nesting level.

**B -- no depth bound.** At 200 and at 20,000 nests the unbounded skipper still
REJECTS, with `ErrTruncated` instead of `ErrDepth`: the buffer runs out before
the stack does, so the cheap case checks the REASON and not the crash. At
200,000 it prints `Stack overflow.` and aborts with SIGABRT, which .NET cannot
catch and no `try` can survive. That is why the committed gate now carries a
200,000-nest case as well as the 200 one: the small case checks the error code,
the large one checks that there is still a process to report it. With the bound
the same 400 KB costs 100 frames and returns `ErrDepth`.

**C -- the group arm added over the 32-bit arm, the core's own near-miss, and it
reproduces exactly.** `groups` reports 0 failures. `conformance` reports 152
checks and 0 failures. The corpus group vectors and byte identity BOTH say the
decoder is fine. The only gate that catches it is `harness unknown` -- this
slice's own hand-built unknown-field vectors, whose `elem-i32` row goes FAIL --
and that is the suite it would have been easiest to think superseded now that
there is a corpus. It is not superseded: it covers the four wire types a proto3
writer can produce and the corpus group vectors cover the fifth, and neither set
covers the other.

