Reading the crossing columns. THE TWO COLUMNS ARE THE SAME QUANTITY, and
this slice said otherwise once and was wrong; the correction is here so the
report does not repeat it.

  core (R5) is the CORE's own counters, read through ak_enc_counters. Zero
    unless the core was built with --features count.
  host is this binding's own tally of the calls it issued.
  They AGREE, entry point for entry point. The only quantity in one and not
    the other is a TRANSCODER invocation, which the core counts as reverse
    and a host tally cannot see -- and this binding stages its strings as
    UTF-8 with ak_tc_bytes(), a pointer INTO the core, so it makes none.

  The gap this slice once reported against the rust slice was a CHUNK SIZE.
  Their host hands the codec 150 elements per ak_elem_* call; this one hands
  over the whole run. Set AK_CHUNK=150 and the columns match to the digit,
  at a cost of 0.7 percent, which is inside the spread. See JOURNAL.md 19.
  They are CONSTANT in the element count, in BOTH directions, whether the
  payload carries 4 elements or 1,000. The 5 and 4 cover an encode, a decode
  and a re-encode:
    encode     2 forward (ak_encode_*, ak_elem_*)  1 reverse (loop_results)
    decode     1 forward (ak_decode_*)             2 reverse (apply, add_results)
  ResultRaw is a LEAF, so encode hands the whole run over in one ak_elem_*
  call and decode gets it back in one add_results. That is ABI v1's batching
  predicate doing the thing it exists for, on .NET.

