################ what this says ################

1. **The D7 core changed nothing for this slice.** 152 checks 0 failures on
   each of the three arms, the core-ffi arm green on M1 and on all five M2
   payloads, and the loaded object confirmed as the one cargo just wrote. The
   change is in the unknown-field skip, which no payload in `ffi/schema`
   exercises, so no movement was expected and none was found.

2. **This slice had the same defect one level up, and the corpus is what found
   it.** Not a review, not byte identity, not the slice's own hand-built
   unknown-field vectors: the corpus, because it is the only oracle in the
   branch that is not generated from the description the decoder is generated
   from. The finding in `ffi/findings/csharp.md` says exactly this and it is
   right.

3. **THIS SLICE IS STILL NOT A CORPUS CONSUMER, and the cost of becoming one is
   nameable.** `ffi/corpus/CONTRACT.md` obliges a consumer to generate its codec
   from `generated/corpus.proto`, parse all 287 accept vectors, project each one
   to JSON, re-encode it to one of the accepted forms, and watch all 49 reject
   vectors fail. This slice's generator drives off `ffi/schema/emit/shapes.py`
   and has **no .proto front end at all**, so conformance means teaching it a
   second one -- a work unit, not an afternoon. What ran here is the five
   vectors the defect is about, read under a root this slice does have, and it
   is labelled as the narrow thing it is rather than as a corpus pass.

   Two of the five are read under a substituted root. The corpus reads
   `X-group-unterminated` and `X-group-mismatched-end` under `WireZoo`, which
   this slice does not generate; their only top-level field is tag 120, which is
   equally unknown to `ListResultsResponse`, so the substitution exercises the
   skipper and nothing else. Said in the harness source as well as here.

4. **A reject vector passing is not evidence.** Both X- vectors passed before
   the fix, because an unhandled wire type is also an error. The accept vectors
   are what failed, and a slice that had only run the reject half would have
   reported a pass on a decoder that rejects a third of the group class.

   The reject vectors earn their place against a DIFFERENT wrong implementation,
   which is why variant A above exists: the one that has a group case and gets
   it wrong. That is the implementation somebody writes after being told to add
   case 3, and `X-group-mismatched-end` is the only thing in this tree that
   catches it.

5. **No single gate covers the skipper, and the three that do cover it do not
   overlap.** Variant C is the proof: byte identity and the corpus group vectors
   both pass a decoder that has silently lost its 32-bit arm, and only this
   slice's own hand-built unknown-field vectors fail. Byte identity cannot reach
   unknown fields at all; the corpus group vectors reach wire type 3; the
   hand-built vectors reach 0, 1, 2 and 5. Removing any of the three would leave
   a hole, and the temptation after acquiring a corpus is to retire the
   hand-built set.

################ what is NOT measured here ################

- Corpus conformance, per paragraph 3. 331 of the 336 vectors are not run.
- Timings. Nothing in this stage is a performance change: the group path is
  never taken by any payload in `ffi/schema` and the skipper is one `switch`
  arm longer in a loop that runs only on unknown fields.
- Nothing in the skipper, now. `MapForms.Skip` was going to be left alone with a
  comment saying it is a harness rewriter that only ever walks bytes this slice
  emitted, where proto3 cannot produce a group. That reasoning is true and it is
  also exactly what was believed about the facade's skipper, so it was fixed
  instead: same field-number match, same 100-nest bound, throwing rather than
  returning a code because it is a harness helper and a loud stop is what it
  wants.
